Expose libsecp's own software field multiply and square, for benchmarking ONLY.

⛔ EXPERIMENTAL, OPT-IN, AND IT MOVES METHOD_ID. Applied only when HAZYNC_FIELD_BENCH=1.
⚠ Adds two functions and changes NO existing code path. Nothing in consensus calls them.

The question this answers: `fe_mul_inner` + `fe_sqr_inner` are a MEASURED 86.4% of the stock block,
and routing them to the bigint2 coprocessor would keep every Core algorithm intact -- same wNAF, same
GLV, same ECDSA logic -- changing only the modular multiply. That is the posture of patches/0002,
which is already accepted in both modes at 3.4% of guest compute, and arguably a SMALLER concession
than #139, which replaces the scalar-multiplication strategy itself.

Whether it is worth taking depends entirely on how a coprocessor field multiply compares with
libsecp's software field_10x26, and that has never been measured. Benchmarking against a
reimplementation would prove nothing, so this exposes libsecp's real functions.

Both loops chain their result into the next iteration so nothing hoists, and return a byte of the
final value so nothing is discarded as dead.

--- a/src/secp256k1.c
+++ b/src/secp256k1.c
@@ -833,3 +833,30 @@
 #ifdef ENABLE_MODULE_ELLSWIFT
 # include "modules/ellswift/main_impl.h"
 #endif
+
+#ifdef HAZYNC_FIELD_BENCH
+/* Time libsecp's OWN software field multiply and square, so the coprocessor can be compared against
+ * the real thing rather than against a reimplementation. Chained so nothing can be hoisted out of
+ * the loop, and the result is returned so it cannot be discarded as dead. */
+int hazync_bench_fe_mul(unsigned int n) {
+    secp256k1_fe a, b, r;
+    unsigned char o[32];
+    unsigned int i;
+    secp256k1_fe_set_int(&a, 3);
+    secp256k1_fe_set_int(&b, 5);
+    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { secp256k1_fe_mul(&r, &a, &b); a = r; }
+    secp256k1_fe_normalize_var(&a);
+    secp256k1_fe_get_b32(o, &a);
+    return (int)o[0];
+}
+int hazync_bench_fe_sqr(unsigned int n) {
+    secp256k1_fe a, r;
+    unsigned char o[32];
+    unsigned int i;
+    secp256k1_fe_set_int(&a, 7);
+    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { secp256k1_fe_sqr(&r, &a); a = r; }
+    secp256k1_fe_normalize_var(&a);
+    secp256k1_fe_get_b32(o, &a);
+    return (int)o[0];
+}
+#endif
